		{"id":"523f8a0de091b44f","type":"text","text":"**Computer Science and main projects** #PROGRAMAÇÃO ","x":-122,"y":-200,"width":342,"height":50,"color":"#ffffff"},
		{"id":"0926805362443c9b","type":"text","text":"Study of information:\nHow do you represent it and how do you process it","x":-238,"y":-80,"width":250,"height":80},
		{"id":"c8a5586b5c34e8a8","type":"text","text":"Lecture Notes - https://cs50.harvard.edu/x/notes/0/","x":278,"y":-204,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"2036d21775f22689","type":"text","text":"Computacional Thinking","x":-238,"y":20,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b81c55baba8252e1","type":"text","text":"Problem solving -  sort a global hability","x":-238,"y":100,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"b116f0d01ad53721","type":"text","text":"The way computer undertand:\n1- Unit - base 1 \n2-Binary - Base 2\n3-Bit (a new term for binary)","x":-540,"y":19,"width":285,"height":141},
		{"id":"556b68f425bfd610","type":"text","text":"     BASE 1 - UNIT \nConsider the numbers themselves: ☝️ = 1, ✌️ = 2, …  \nThis system is called **Unary (Base 1)**, where each value is represented by repeating a single unit.  \nIt is simple but inefficient, since large numbers require many symbols, which is why computers do not use Base 1 and instead rely on **Base 2 (Binary)**.","x":-809,"y":62,"width":250,"height":418},
		{"id":"81b569037e6a97ca","type":"text","text":"EXEMPLE TRANSISTORS\n    💡 OFF = 0\n    💡 ON   =   1\nA computer stores and processes information using billions of tiny electronic switches called **transistors**.  \nEach transistor can be in one of two states: **OFF (0)** or **ON (1)**.  \nThese two states form **binary**, the basic language of computers.  \nBy combining many 0s and 1s, computers can represent numbers, letters, images, sounds, and instructions.","x":-247,"y":340,"width":287,"height":440},
		{"id":"e19c12e22de959d2","type":"text","text":"    BASE 10 - DECIMALS\nHumans commonly use **Decimal (Base 10)**, which is based on ten digits: **0–9**.  \nEach position represents a power of 10, making it intuitive for counting and everyday calculations, but not practical for computers, which rely on binary states.","x":-817,"y":513,"width":258,"height":347},
		{"id":"9b5a9e4ef08a1f1d","type":"text","text":"4  - 2 -  1 \nO    O    O ","x":-229,"y":267,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"926b55989864c72c","type":"file","file":"FERRAMENTAS/IMAGES/3 BITS.png","x":88,"y":297,"width":112,"height":75},
		{"id":"da3c384474a067e8","type":"file","file":"FERRAMENTAS/IMAGES/8 BITS.png","x":278,"y":297,"width":199,"height":67},
		{"id":"ae7ff64a2557f0dc","type":"text","text":"0  = 00000\n1  = 00001\n2  = 00010\n3  = 00011\n4  = 00100\n5  = 00101\n6  = 00110\n7  = 00111\n8  = 01000\n9  = 01001\n10 = 01010\n11 = 01011\n12 = 01100\n13 = 01101\n14 = 01110\n15 = 01111\n16 = 10000\n17 = 10001\n18 = 10010\n19 = 10011\n20 = 10100\n\n","x":88,"y":401,"width":132,"height":539},
		{"id":"e179e7867e588c9b","type":"text","text":"#PROGRAMAÇÃO #logica ","x":-358,"y":-314,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"1a4fdd4f8304bf33","type":"text","text":"    BASE 2 - BINARY\nConsiders not just the numbers but the value of each digit, for exemple: \n👆🏽or👇🏽 \nBoth are just one finger, but with different properties: same as \"black and white\", \"0 and 1\"etc\n\nIts different than the decinal sistem, that bases every calculus on the number 10 ","x":-540,"y":220,"width":250,"height":405}
